FINDING 1 -- the strawman collapse (search-INDEPENDENT: stop-dead vs skip-continue):
  On 14 voltage-limited US feeders, switching the old stop-dead baseline to an honest
  skip-continue FCFS walk recovers a median +29% more capacity
  (range +0% to +1420%). The +192%/+1367% margins
  experiments 39/40 reported were measuring joint-vs-STRAWMAN; against a real FCFS
  baseline that capacity was never actually lost. skip-continue seats a median
  95% of all requested kW.

FINDING 2 -- joint (NSGA-II) does NOT beat honest FCFS on these large feeders:
  on 5 size-spanning feeders, joint beats skip-continue on 0/5;
  median joint-vs-skip = -6.0%. Skip-continue already seats ~94% of requested kW, so the
  joint ceiling is close; and NSGA-II under-searches at this budget (no fast optimal
  route exists for this oracle). Either way: no demonstrable joint advantage here.

  INTERPRETATION: voltage-limiting is necessary but NOT sufficient for a joint advantage.
  It needs strong request COUPLING (small/globally-saturated feeders, e.g. EULV +46%),
  which these large real feeders -- demand spread over thousands of buses -- do not provide.

